/-!
# Generic reducedness and smooth comparisons of strict transforms

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 036C, 00EU, 0BFM, 080E, 0805 and 081I
(blueprint: lem-per-generically-reduced-strict-transform-geometry).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Generic stalks are fields, and ordinary blowups along nowhere dense finite-type ideals
preserve generic reducedness and generic stalks. Ambient strict transforms are these same
ordinary blowups, with their scheme structures.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 00EU, 0BFM and 080E
(blueprint: lem-per-generically-reduced-strict-transform-geometry).

Proof (node steps 1--4):
1. Identify a generic stalk with localization at a minimal prime using
lem-weil-pbc-spec-irreducible-components-minimal-primes and
lem-weil-pbc-spec-local-ring-canonical-morphism. The maximal ideal is nilpotent elementwise
(lem-weil-pbc-minimal-prime-reduced-localization-field); reducedness makes it zero, so the
stalk is a field and is regular (lem-weil-pbc-field-regular-ring).
2. On a Noetherian affine chart, a closed subset with empty interior contains no component
(lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-finitely-many-minimal-primes). Hence every generic point lies in
the complement of the center. The blowup is proper and thus locally of finite type, so
pqf-lem-locnoeth-lft gives local Noetherianity of the blowup.
3. On a blowup chart the extended ideal is generated by a nonzerodivisor
(lemma-nagata-chart-extended-ideal-principal and lemma-nagata-chart-generator-regular).
No minimal prime contains that generator (lem-pc-minimal-prime-avoids-nonzerodivisor).
Thus every component generic point lies over the complement, densely in its component.
4. The complement isomorphism thm-nagata-global-blowup-complement identifies the generic
points and their stalk maps. The generic stalks upstairs are reduced. Finally Tag 080E,
lemma-nagata-strict-transform-ambient-closed, identifies the scheme-theoretic strict
transform with the blowup of the extended ideal, without reducing either scheme. -/
theorem genericallyReduced_blowup_geometry (X : Scheme.{u}) [IsLocallyNoetherian X]
    (hX : ∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents X, ∀ η : X, IsGenericPoint η C →
      _root_.IsReduced (X.presheaf.stalk η)) :
    (∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents X, ∀ η : X, IsGenericPoint η C →
      IsField (X.presheaf.stalk η) ∧ IsRegularLocalRing (X.presheaf.stalk η)) ∧
    (∀ (J : X.IdealSheafData), J.ofFiniteType → interior (J.support : Set X) = ∅ →
      IsLocallyNoetherian J.blowup ∧
      (∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents J.blowup, ∀ ξ : J.blowup, IsGenericPoint ξ C →
        _root_.IsReduced (J.blowup.presheaf.stalk ξ)) ∧
      (∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents J.blowup,
        Dense ((Subtype.val : C → J.blowup) ⁻¹'
          (J.blowupMap ⁻¹' (J.support.compl : Set X)))) ∧
      (∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents J.blowup, ∀ ξ : J.blowup, IsGenericPoint ξ C →
        J.blowupMap ξ ∈ J.support.compl ∧
        (∃ D ∈ irreducibleComponents X, IsGenericPoint (J.blowupMap ξ) D) ∧
        IsIso (J.blowupMap.stalkMap ξ)) ∧
      (∀ D ∈ irreducibleComponents X, ∀ η : X, IsGenericPoint η D →
        ∃! ξ : J.blowup, J.blowupMap ξ = η ∧
          ∃ C ∈ irreducibleComponents J.blowup, IsGenericPoint ξ C) ∧
      (∀ (P : Scheme.{u}) (i : X ⟶ P) [IsClosedImmersion i]
          (I : P.IdealSheafData), I.ofFiniteType → I.comap i = J →
        ∃ e : J.blowup ≅ I.strictTransform i,
          e.hom ≫ strictTransformToSource i I.blowupMap I.support.compl = J.blowupMap ∧
          (e.hom ≫ strictTransformMap i I.blowupMap I.support.compl) ≫ I.blowupMap =
            J.blowupMap ≫ i)) := by
  sorry

/-- Smooth maps preserve generic reducedness and carry component generic points to component
generic points. No surjectivity or reducedness away from those points is assumed.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 036C, 00HS and 0HA1
(blueprint: lem-per-generically-reduced-strict-transform-geometry).

Proof (node steps 5--6): Smoothness gives flatness and local finite presentation, hence
local finite type and local Noetherianity by pqf-lem-locnoeth-lft. In affine neighborhoods
of a generic point, flat going down (lem-sc-flat-going-down) makes its contracted prime
minimal. The downstairs generic stalk is a field by step 1. The smooth fibre is reduced
(sbc-lem-smooth-geometrically-reduced). The fibre-stalk quotient isomorphism
lem-sbc-scheme-fibre-stalk-quotient then identifies the upstairs stalk with its quotient by
the extension of the zero maximal ideal. Thus it is reduced. Since generic points map to
generic points, the inverse image of any nowhere dense center again has empty interior. -/
theorem genericallyReduced_smooth_geometry {X Y : Scheme.{u}} [IsLocallyNoetherian X]
    (hX : ∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents X, ∀ η : X, IsGenericPoint η C →
      _root_.IsReduced (X.presheaf.stalk η)) (v : Y ⟶ X) [Smooth v] :
    IsLocallyNoetherian Y ∧
    (∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents Y, ∀ ξ : Y, IsGenericPoint ξ C →
      _root_.IsReduced (Y.presheaf.stalk ξ) ∧
        ∃ D ∈ irreducibleComponents X, IsGenericPoint (v ξ) D) ∧
    (∀ J : X.IdealSheafData, J.ofFiniteType → interior (J.support : Set X) = ∅ →
      interior ((J.comap v).support : Set Y) = ∅ ∧
        IsIso (J.blowupFlatBaseChange v)) := by
  sorry

/-- Every finite ambient blowup/strict-transform diagram admits its uncontracted smooth
pullback, with canonical flat comparisons. All earlier ideals are retained at every level,
including identity blowups. Comparison maps are unique from their initial maps, giving
compatibility with identities and further pullback.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0805, 080E, 081I and 01JU
(blueprint: lem-per-generically-reduced-strict-transform-geometry).

Proof (node steps 7--9):
7. Smooth maps are flat and remain smooth under base change. Apply the canonical comparison
of def-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change-map and thm-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change at each
ambient stage, and lem-trf-strict-transform-flat-etale-base-change to the closed strict
transform. Its generic-point hypothesis follows from step 5. The chart maps and overlaps
are the coefficientwise ones of lem-sfb-flat-pullback-blowup-chart-restrictions.
8. Induct on the number of stages starting from the prescribed initial pullback. At each
step extend every earlier ideal. The equality of successive and composite ideal extensions
is the generator calculation in lem-pc-closed-subscheme-base-change-ideal-image. On charts
both successive coefficient maps send a fraction to its coefficientwise image under the
composite; hence comparisons commute with further pullback. Uniqueness of maps to a blowup
over the base, followed by the closed-immersion property of each strict transform, gives
uniqueness of all stage maps from the two initial maps.
9. A unit ideal has empty center and its complement is the whole scheme. The complement
isomorphism makes that stage an identity. Extending an earlier ideal along this isomorphism
still retains that ideal; no ideal is discarded when an intrinsic blowup happens to be an
identity for any other reason. Length zero, empty schemes and unit ideals cause no exception. -/
theorem genericallyReduced_strictTransform_smooth_diagrams
    (n : ℕ) (D : FiniteEmbeddedBlowupDiagram.{u} n)
    [IsLocallyNoetherian (D.intrinsic.obj (op 0))]
    (hD : ∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents (D.intrinsic.obj (op 0)),
      ∀ η : D.intrinsic.obj (op 0), IsGenericPoint η C →
        _root_.IsReduced ((D.intrinsic.obj (op 0)).presheaf.stalk η)) :
    (∀ (W : Scheme.{u}) (v : W ⟶ D.ambient.obj (op 0)) [Smooth v],
      ∃ (E : FiniteEmbeddedBlowupDiagram.{u} n)
        (c : E.Comparison D) (e : E.ambient.obj (op 0) ≅ W),
        c.ambient.app (op 0) = e.hom ≫ v) ∧
    (∀ (E : FiniteEmbeddedBlowupDiagram.{u} n) (c : E.Comparison D)
        (j : Fin n) (k : Fin (n + 1)) (hjk : j.castSucc ≤ k),
      ((D.center j).comap (D.ambient.map (homOfLE hjk).op)).comap
          (c.ambient.app (op k)) =
        (E.center j).comap (E.ambient.map (homOfLE hjk).op) ∧
      (((D.center j).comap (D.inclusion.app (op j.castSucc))).comap
          (D.intrinsic.map (homOfLE hjk).op)).comap (c.intrinsic.app (op k)) =
        ((E.center j).comap (E.inclusion.app (op j.castSucc))).comap
          (E.intrinsic.map (homOfLE hjk).op)) ∧
    (∀ (E : FiniteEmbeddedBlowupDiagram.{u} n) (c : E.Comparison D) (j : Fin n),
      (E.center j).support = ⊥ →
        IsIso (E.ambient.map (homOfLE (le_of_lt j.castSucc_lt_succ)).op) ∧
        IsIso (E.intrinsic.map (homOfLE (le_of_lt j.castSucc_lt_succ)).op)) ∧
    (∀ (E : FiniteEmbeddedBlowupDiagram.{u} n) (c d : E.Comparison D),
      c.ambient.app (op 0) = d.ambient.app (op 0) →
      c.intrinsic.app (op 0) = d.intrinsic.app (op 0) →
      c.ambient = d.ambient ∧ c.intrinsic = d.intrinsic) ∧
    (∃ c : D.Comparison D,
      c.ambient = 𝟙 D.ambient ∧ c.intrinsic = 𝟙 D.intrinsic) ∧
    (∀ (E F : FiniteEmbeddedBlowupDiagram.{u} n) (c : E.Comparison D)
        (d : F.Comparison E),
      ∃ e : F.Comparison D,
        e.ambient = d.ambient ≫ c.ambient ∧ e.intrinsic = d.intrinsic ≫ c.intrinsic) ∧
    (∀ (E : FiniteEmbeddedBlowupDiagram.{u} n) (c : E.Comparison D),
      ∀ k : Fin (n + 1),
      (∀ j : Fin n, j.succ ≤ k →
        interior (((D.center j).comap (D.inclusion.app (op j.castSucc))).support :
          Set (D.intrinsic.obj (op j.castSucc))) = ∅) →
      IsLocallyNoetherian (E.intrinsic.obj (op k)) ∧
      ∀ C ∈ irreducibleComponents (E.intrinsic.obj (op k)),
        ∀ ξ : E.intrinsic.obj (op k), IsGenericPoint ξ C →
          _root_.IsReduced ((E.intrinsic.obj (op k)).presheaf.stalk ξ)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
